<div>
    <div class=3D"cmp-text ">
        <h3>How the macroeconomic environment could impact the banking indu=
stry</h3>
<p>Banks are likely to enter 2026 on a relatively strong footing, following=
 resilient earnings in the first three quarters of 2025. However, they may =
face some headwinds in net interest income in 2026, driven largely by lower=
 rates and a slowing economy.</p>
<p>Net interest income improved by 4% in the first half of 2025 after a dec=
line in 2024.<sup><a id=3D"endnote-sup-11" href=3D"https://www.deloitte.com=
/us/en/insights/industry/financial-services/financial-services-industry-out=
looks/banking-industry-outlook.html#endnote-11" aria-label=3D"Endnotes refe=
rence number 11">11</a></sup> However, net interest income growth in 2026 c=
ould be modest (figure 1), likely driven by lower loan yields. Deposit cost=
s, however, should continue to drop. The average cost of interest-bearing d=
eposits had already declined to 2.5% in the first six months of 2025.<sup><=
a id=3D"endnote-sup-12" href=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/ind=
ustry/financial-services/financial-services-industry-outlooks/banking-indus=
try-outlook.html#endnote-12" aria-label=3D"Endnotes reference number 12">12=
</a></sup> But deposit betas may remain relatively low, particularly for re=
gional banks, as the competition for deposits remains high.<sup><a id=3D"en=
dnote-sup-13" href=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/industry/fina=
ncial-services/financial-services-industry-outlooks/banking-industry-outloo=
k.html#endnote-13" aria-label=3D"Endnotes reference number 13">13</a></sup>=
</p>

    </div>
</div>

