   =20
    <div id=3D"container-12f37f2d44" class=3D"cmp-container">
       =20
        <div class=3D"text cmp-di-text pl-0 pr-0 pl-mob-2 pr-mob-2 aem-Grid=
Column--phone--12 cmp-di-text--line-height-large cmp-di-text-bullets">

   =20

<div class=3D"hide-no-results-error-msg">
    <img src=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/content/dam/insights/icons/svg/ico=
n-alert.svg"> =20
</div>
<div>
    <div class=3D"cmp-text ">
        <p>On the capital front, US banks remain well capitalized=E2=80=94t=
he average common equity tier 1 ratio has remained above 14% over the last =
five years.<sup><a id=3D"endnote-sup-22" href=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/u=
s/en/insights/industry/financial-services/financial-services-industry-outlo=
oks/banking-industry-outlook.html#endnote-22" aria-label=3D"Endnotes refere=
nce number 22">22</a></sup> In addition, the proposed regulations to modify=
 the enhanced Supplementary Leverage Ratio could benefit large banks by red=
ucing the aggregate capital requirements to a range of 3.5% to 4.5%, down f=
rom the current range of 5% to 6%.<sup><a id=3D"endnote-sup-23" href=3D"htt=
ps://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/industry/financial-services/financial-=
services-industry-outlooks/banking-industry-outlook.html#endnote-23" aria-l=
abel=3D"Endnotes reference number 23">23</a></sup></p>
<p>There was excess capital of over $250 billion among the top 20 US banks =
through the first half of 2025.<sup><a id=3D"endnote-sup-24" href=3D"https:=
//www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/industry/financial-services/financial-ser=
vices-industry-outlooks/banking-industry-outlook.html#endnote-24" aria-labe=
l=3D"Endnotes reference number 24">24</a></sup> As a result, banks may cont=
inue returning capital through dividends and share repurchases and use some=
 of the proceeds to fund growth and AI ambitions.</p>

    </div>
</div>

   =20

</div>
<div class=3D"text cmp-di-text pl-0 pr-0 pl-mob-2 pr-mob-2 aem-GridColumn--=
phone--12 cmp-di-text--line-height-large cmp-di-text-bullets">

   =20

<div class=3D"hide-no-results-error-msg">
    <img src=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/content/dam/insights/icons/svg/ico=
n-alert.svg"> =20
</div>
<div>
    <div class=3D"cmp-text ">
        <h3>Rate trajectories around the world</h3>
<p>Many European banks are exhibiting a strong comeback. They have outperfo=
rmed many of their global peers=E2=80=94with a 45% year-to-date increase in=
 share-price returns through August 2025<sup><a id=3D"endnote-sup-25" href=
=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/industry/financial-services/fin=
ancial-services-industry-outlooks/banking-industry-outlook.html#endnote-25"=
 aria-label=3D"Endnotes reference number 25">25</a></sup>=E2=80=94even with=
 current global macroeconomic tensions.<sup><a id=3D"endnote-sup-26" href=
=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/industry/financial-services/fin=
ancial-services-industry-outlooks/banking-industry-outlook.html#endnote-26"=
 aria-label=3D"Endnotes reference number 26">26</a></sup> Looking forward, =
they may see a pickup in loan growth amid falling rates and continued suppo=
rt from noninterest income. There could be mild deterioration because of tr=
ade tariffs, but it would largely remain under control. After many years of=
 stagnation, European banks may enjoy improved economic growth=E2=80=94eith=
er organically or through consolidation=E2=80=94in the coming years.<sup><a=
 id=3D"endnote-sup-27" href=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/indu=
stry/financial-services/financial-services-industry-outlooks/banking-indust=
ry-outlook.html#endnote-27" aria-label=3D"Endnotes reference number 27">27<=
/a></sup></p>
<p>Meanwhile, Asia-Pacific banks are likely to show strong growth in emergi=
ng markets, though challenges persist in certain economies, especially thos=
e with exposure to US trade tariffs.<sup><a id=3D"endnote-sup-28" href=3D"h=
ttps://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/industry/financial-services/financia=
l-services-industry-outlooks/banking-industry-outlook.html#endnote-28" aria=
-label=3D"Endnotes reference number 28">28</a></sup> Capital market activit=
ies recorded a slump in July, with banks in the region raising $6.6 billion=
, one of the lowest totals in the past year.<sup><a id=3D"endnote-sup-29" h=
ref=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/industry/financial-services/=
financial-services-industry-outlooks/banking-industry-outlook.html#endnote-=
29" aria-label=3D"Endnotes reference number 29">29</a></sup></p>

    </div>
</div>

   =20

</div>
<div class=3D"text cmp-di-text pt-1 pb-1 pl-mob-2 pr-mob-2 aem-GridColumn--=
phone--12 cmp-di-text--line-height-large cmp-di-text-bullets">

   =20

<div class=3D"hide-no-results-error-msg">
    <img src=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/content/dam/insights/icons/svg/ico=
n-alert.svg"> =20
</div>
<div>
    <div id=3D"disruption" class=3D"cmp-text ">
        <h2>Banks brace for potential deposit disruption while assessing st=
ablecoin opportunities</h2>
<p>Stablecoins could herald a new era of money, presenting both challenges =
and opportunities for banks and payment companies. 2026 could be a pivotal =
year to develop strategies and address the risks related to stablecoins. In=
 response, banks will likely need to bolster their infrastructure and capab=
ilities as alternatives to deposits and payment rails emerge.</p>
<p>The July 2025 Guiding and Establishing National Innovation for US Stable=
coins (GENIUS) Act created a legislative framework for payment stablecoins =
(PSCs) in the United States, providing regulatory clarity and opening the d=
oor for traditional banks to be involved in tokenized digital assets. The b=
ill is part of a larger effort designed to help the United States become th=
e =E2=80=9Ccrypto capital of the world.=E2=80=9D<sup><a id=3D"endnote-sup-3=
0" href=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/industry/financial-servi=
ces/financial-services-industry-outlooks/banking-industry-outlook.html#endn=
ote-30" aria-label=3D"Endnotes reference number 30">30</a></sup> This and o=
ther potential legislation should spur further digital asset adoption by co=
rporates and consumers alike.<sup><a id=3D"endnote-sup-31" href=3D"https://=
www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/industry/financial-services/financial-servi=
ces-industry-outlooks/banking-industry-outlook.html#endnote-31" aria-label=
=3D"Endnotes reference number 31">31</a></sup>Institutional demand is alrea=
dy evident: Nearly one in four chief financial officers surveyed expect to =
use cryptocurrencies within the next two years as a payment method or corpo=
rate investment, according to Deloitte=E2=80=99s second quarter 2025 North =
American CFO Signals survey.<sup><a id=3D"endnote-sup-32" href=3D"https://w=
ww.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/industry/financial-services/financial-servic=
es-industry-outlooks/banking-industry-outlook.html#endnote-32" aria-label=
=3D"Endnotes reference number 32">32</a></sup></p>
<p>Due to their unique attributes, PSCs could play a more formidable role i=
n financial services. They can offer faster and cheaper payments and settle=
ments than traditional financial infrastructure. Unlike other types of digi=
tal assets, PSCs are backed by reserves of fiat-currency assets, like US tr=
easuries, to maintain a one-to-one peg ratio.</p>
<h3>Tokenized deposits: An alternative to payment stablecoins?</h3>
<p>As some banks consider their options with stablecoins, an alternative so=
lution has also emerged: tokenized deposits. Like PSCs, they also provide i=
nstant settlement, lower transaction costs, and programmability for custome=
rs of the same bank. Uniquely, tokenized deposits have the advantages of na=
tive cash settlement and the ability to pay interest, and they may also be =
used as payments for other digital assets and as on-chain collateral.<sup><=
a id=3D"endnote-sup-33" href=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/ind=
ustry/financial-services/financial-services-industry-outlooks/banking-indus=
try-outlook.html#endnote-33" aria-label=3D"Endnotes reference number 33">33=
</a></sup> Unlike PSCs, they remain within the existing regulatory framewor=
k of a bank: These deposits remain liabilities of banks, subject to the sam=
e capital management and regulatory oversight.</p>
<p>Notably, tokenized deposits could be a counterstrategy for banks that ar=
e cautious about the impact of PSCs on deposit funding. Some banks, includi=
ng J.P. Morgan and Citibank, are already offering tokenized deposits to cli=
ents alongside their efforts with PSCs.<sup><a id=3D"endnote-sup-34" href=
=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/industry/financial-services/fin=
ancial-services-industry-outlooks/banking-industry-outlook.html#endnote-34"=
 aria-label=3D"Endnotes reference number 34">34</a></sup></p>
<h3>Drivers of PSC growth and threats to deposits and fees</h3>
<p>The primary appeal of stablecoins has typically been tied to their role =
in crypto trading. As price-stable assets, PSCs have often become the prefe=
rred medium for moving between more volatile crypto tokens and for serving =
as a tool for efficient settlement, arbitrage, and collateral. Moreover, th=
eir deep liquidity and predictable value have made PSCs the default choice =
for on- and off-ramp conversions, establishing them as a bridge between tra=
ditional finance and the crypto economy.<sup><a id=3D"endnote-sup-35" href=
=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/industry/financial-services/fin=
ancial-services-industry-outlooks/banking-industry-outlook.html#endnote-35"=
 aria-label=3D"Endnotes reference number 35">35</a></sup></p>
<p>While the current market for PSCs is relatively small, various industry =
forecasts suggest potential growth from $250 billion to a bullish estimate =
of $3.7 trillion, or a bearish estimate of around $500 billion by 2030.<sup=
><a id=3D"endnote-sup-36" href=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/i=
ndustry/financial-services/financial-services-industry-outlooks/banking-ind=
ustry-outlook.html#endnote-36" aria-label=3D"Endnotes reference number 36">=
36</a></sup> The growth of PSCs could pose a threat to bank deposits, with =
more than $1 trillion potentially at risk (figure 3).</p>
<p>Flows are anticipated to come from three buckets: corporate working capi=
tal held in low-yield transaction accounts; retail transaction balances for=
 24/7 peer-to-peer and commerce use; and cross-border settlement float curr=
ently trapped in nostro or vostro networks. While the GENIUS Act prohibits =
stablecoin issuers from paying interest, non-issuers can offer =E2=80=9Crew=
ards,=E2=80=9D potentially spurring demand.<sup><a id=3D"endnote-sup-37" hr=
ef=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/industry/financial-services/f=
inancial-services-industry-outlooks/banking-industry-outlook.html#endnote-3=
7" aria-label=3D"Endnotes reference number 37">37</a></sup> Given the signi=
ficant potential impact of PSCs on deposit flows, banks could face tighter =
liquidity and reduced lending capacity.</p>

    </div>
</div>

   =20

</div>

       =20
    </div>

</div>
