n-alert.svg"> =20
</div>
<div>
    <div id=3D"Modernizing" class=3D"cmp-text ">
        <h2>Redoubling the commitment to a modern, AI-ready infrastructure<=
/h2>
<p>Many banks have made sizable progress in modernizing their data infrastr=
ucture. In particular, moving core data to the cloud<sup><a id=3D"endnote-s=
up-62" href=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/industry/financial-s=
ervices/financial-services-industry-outlooks/banking-industry-outlook.html#=
endnote-62" aria-label=3D"Endnotes reference number 62">62</a></sup> has he=
lped strengthen their data management practices. However, without an AI-gra=
de data infrastructure, models may underperform, gen AI pilots could stall<=
sup><a id=3D"endnote-sup-63" href=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insight=
s/industry/financial-services/financial-services-industry-outlooks/banking-=
industry-outlook.html#endnote-63" aria-label=3D"Endnotes reference number 6=
3">63</a></sup> or fall short of regulatory standards and customer expectat=
ions, and future agentic AI initiatives may fail to launch. As AI moves fro=
m pilots to enterprise scale, building a more resilient and future-proof da=
ta architecture could be critically urgent.</p>
<h3>What have banks done to become AI-ready?</h3>
<p>Our analysis indicates that data readiness for AI among US banks is high=
ly uneven=E2=80=94both across banks and within the same institutions.<sup><=
a id=3D"endnote-sup-64" href=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/ind=
ustry/financial-services/financial-services-industry-outlooks/banking-indus=
try-outlook.html#endnote-64" aria-label=3D"Endnotes reference number 64">64=
</a></sup> While cloud migration should streamline and organize data better=
,<sup><a id=3D"endnote-sup-65" href=3D"https://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insig=
hts/industry/financial-services/financial-services-industry-outlooks/bankin=
g-industry-outlook.html#endnote-65" aria-label=3D"Endnotes reference number=
 65">65</a></sup> =E2=80=9Cbad=E2=80=9D data may have been migrated, or dat=
a may still exist in silos within the cloud.</p>
<p>Additionally, US banks that prepared their data for regulatory complianc=
e should have helped with AI-readiness<sup><a id=3D"endnote-sup-66" href=3D=
"https://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/industry/financial-services/financ=
ial-services-industry-outlooks/banking-industry-outlook.html#endnote-66" ar=
ia-label=3D"Endnotes reference number 66">66</a></sup> with cleaner, more t=
raceable, and better-governed data. For example, capital and liquidity rule=
s have forced some banks to consolidate risk data and establish lineage; st=
ress-testing regimes such as the Comprehensive Capital Analysis and Review =
(CCAR) and the Dodd-Frank Act Stress Test (DFAST) have driven more timely, =
auditable data sets; and AML and sanctions reporting have required standard=
ized customer and transaction data.<sup><a id=3D"endnote-sup-67" href=3D"ht=
tps://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/industry/financial-services/financial=
-services-industry-outlooks/banking-industry-outlook.html#endnote-67" aria-=
label=3D"Endnotes reference number 67">67</a></sup> But, these investments =
often remain siloed, serving only their original compliance mandate rather =
than being scaled into a foundation that could power AI across the enterpri=
se.</p>
<p>US banks with previous experience in robotic process automation (RPA) an=
d AI should have established data catalogs, clear lineage, quality metadata=
, new controls, and continuous quality monitoring to improve the accuracy, =
calibration, and stability of AI models. For instance, banks like BNY are u=
sing external sources such as the National Institute of Standards and Techn=
ology=E2=80=99s AI risk management framework to align their use of data acr=
oss the AI life cycle.<sup><a id=3D"endnote-sup-68" href=3D"https://www.del=
oitte.com/us/en/insights/industry/financial-services/financial-services-ind=
ustry-outlooks/banking-industry-outlook.html#endnote-68" aria-label=3D"Endn=
otes reference number 68">68</a></sup></p>
<p>Others are treating data as a product. J.P. Morgan=E2=80=99s Fusion, for=
 example, offers standardized, aggregated private-markets data sets to inst=
itutional investors.<sup><a id=3D"endnote-sup-69" href=3D"https://www.deloi=
tte.com/us/en/insights/industry/financial-services/financial-services-indus=
try-outlooks/banking-industry-outlook.html#endnote-69" aria-label=3D"Endnot=
es reference number 69">69</a></sup> This data-as-a-product approach can he=
lp enable consistency, discoverability, ownership, and reusability of data =
in both internal and external environments. It can also create licensed, hi=
gh-quality training and RAG-ready retrieval sets.</p>
<h3>What it means to have AI-ready data</h3>
<p>AI is redefining what =E2=80=9Cgood data=E2=80=9D means in banking. Figu=
re 6 summarizes some of the key pillars of an AI-ready data architecture.</=
p>
<p>Banks=E2=80=99 AI readiness is often slowed by the data foundations that=
 models depend on. Poor infrastructure can result in data sprawl, vulnerabi=
lity, and limited data-led innovation, limiting model efficacy. In addition=
, data silos often leave training sets incomplete and biased. The impact is=
 evident. In Deloitte=E2=80=99s 2024 Banking &amp; Capital Markets Data and=
 Analytics Market Survey,<sup><a id=3D"endnote-sup-70" href=3D"https://www.=
deloitte.com/us/en/insights/industry/financial-services/financial-services-=
industry-outlooks/banking-industry-outlook.html#endnote-70" aria-label=3D"E=
ndnotes reference number 70">70</a> </sup>more than 90% of data users in ba=
nks reported that the data they need is often unavailable or takes too long=
 to retrieve. Data quality also ranked high, with 81% of respondents citing=
 it as a top challenge.<sup><a id=3D"endnote-sup-71" href=3D"https://www.de=
loitte.com/us/en/insights/industry/financial-services/financial-services-in=
dustry-outlooks/banking-industry-outlook.html#endnote-71" aria-label=3D"End=
notes reference number 71">71</a></sup></p>

